var RG = window.RG;

// Round 11: "Living it out" — deep questions, set 4 (suffering, sin, prayer, faith, works, testing)

RG.lvQA4 = [
  {
    id: "why-not-protect",
    q: "Why is God not helping or protecting us voluntarily or automatically? Would you see your own child like that and stay quiet?",
    short: "This is the hardest question in the faith, and Scripture lets us ask it out loud. Christians answer it in different ways (free will, non-coercive love, God suffering with us, honest mystery), but every faithful answer starts where Jesus did: weeping at a grave, not explaining it away.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "First: the question is allowed",
        body: [
          "If you have watched someone you love suffer and asked “Where were you, God?”, you are not faithless. You are standing in a long biblical line. The Psalms ask “How long, Yahweh? Will you forget me forever?” (Ps 13:1). Job argues with God for chapters. Jesus himself cries from the cross, “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?” (Mark 15:34, quoting Ps 22:1).",
          "Your comparison with a parent is a fair one. Jesus used it himself: “how much more will your Father who is in heaven give good things to those who ask him!” (Matt 7:11). If a loving human parent would rush to protect a child, it is honest to ask why God does not always do the same. Any answer that tells you to stop asking is not a good answer.",
          "And one thing must be said clearly before anything else: suffering is not proof that the sufferer did something wrong. Jesus rejected that idea (John 9:2-3; Luke 13:1-5), and God rebuked Job’s friends for blaming him (Job 42:7)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What many of us were taught",
        body: [
          "Many Christians were taught a short answer: “God is in control, everything happens for a reason.” In its strongest form (often in Reformed theology), God ordains all that happens, including evil, for purposes we cannot see, and his goodness is beyond our judgment (Rom 9:20; Isa 55:8-9). Others were taught a different short answer: “If you had enough faith, God would have protected you.”",
          "The first answer protects God’s power but can make God look like the author of every cancer and every abuse. The second answer protects God’s goodness but quietly puts the blame on the person who is hurting. Both can leave wounded people more alone. The relational gospel looks for an answer that keeps God’s love at the centre and never blames the victim."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "How Christians actually answer it (fairly stated)",
        body: [
          "<strong>Free will.</strong> Love requires real freedom. If God overrode every harmful choice, people would not be real partners in love. Much suffering comes from human choices, and God does not turn us into puppets. Critics reply: that explains abuse, but not earthquakes or childhood cancer.",
          "<strong>Non-coercive love.</strong> Thomas Jay Oord (open and relational theology) goes further. He argues that God’s love is by nature “uncontrolling”: God <em>can’t</em> control creatures, not just <em>won’t</em>, and so God cannot prevent evil “by acting alone”. This answers your parent question directly: God is not standing back while able to stop it. But it is strongly debated. Most traditional theologians, and many relational-gospel writers too, say it gives up too much of God’s power and makes the miracles in Scripture hard to explain.",
          "<strong>Cruciform presence.</strong> Writers like Brad Jersak and many others point to the cross: God does not watch suffering from far away. In Christ, God enters it, bears it and defeats it from the inside (Heb 2:14-18; Isa 53:4). The Spirit groans with us “with groanings which can’t be uttered” (Rom 8:26). On this view God is <em>not</em> quiet. He is with the child, in the pain, working toward resurrection.",
          "<strong>Honest mystery.</strong> Job never receives an explanation (Job 38–42). Many believers in every tradition finally say: we do not know why this happened, but we know who God is in Jesus, and we refuse to call evil good. Creation is “groaning” and not yet healed (Rom 8:19-23); the final answer is a promise, not a theory (Rev 21:4)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer: look at Jesus at the grave",
        body: [
          "John 11 is the clearest picture. Jesus knows he is about to raise Lazarus, yet when he sees Mary weeping, he is “deeply moved in spirit” and “Jesus wept” (John 11:33-35). He does not lecture her about God’s plan. He does not say it was for a reason. He grieves with her, and then he acts against death. The relational gospel says: <em>this</em> is what God is like (John 14:9; Heb 1:3).",
          "So the honest relational answer has three parts. God is not the author of evil (Jas 1:13, 17; 1 John 1:5). God is not absent: he weeps, carries and works in every situation, often through people (Rom 8:28 is about God working in all things, not causing all things). And God has not finished: the resurrection of Jesus is the promise that death and harm will not have the last word (1 Cor 15:54-57).",
          "Why God does not intervene more often, or more visibly, remains partly unanswered in Scripture. Be suspicious of anyone who claims to have fully solved it, including us."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The strongest counter-view and a response",
        body: [
          "Reformed and classical theists say: if God is not fully sovereign over evil, then we have no guarantee he can win. Romans 8:28 and Genesis 50:20 (“you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good”) show God governing even evil acts. They warn that a God who “can’t” is not the God of the Exodus or the empty tomb.",
          "A fair response: Scripture does show God bringing good out of evil (Gen 50:20), and Christians in the relational stream agree God will win. The disagreement is over whether God <em>wills</em> each evil or <em>overcomes</em> it. Jesus never treated sickness or death as God’s will to be accepted; he treated them as enemies (Luke 13:16; Acts 10:38). Christians can hold different views here and still worship the same crucified and risen Lord."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do when you are in it",
        body: [
          "Bring your anger and grief to God honestly; lament is prayer (Ps 13; Ps 88). Do not let anyone tell you your pain is your fault or your lack of faith. Sit with people who are suffering instead of explaining their suffering (Rom 12:15; Job 2:13).",
          "Act as God’s hands where you can: protect the vulnerable, report abuse, get medical and professional help, and if anyone is in danger, contact local emergency services first. Faith does not replace protection; it often moves us to provide it.",
          "And hold on to the One who wept. You do not have to understand everything to trust that God is not against you."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["John 11:33-35", "Ps 13:1", "Mark 15:34", "Matt 7:11", "John 9:2-3", "Luke 13:1-5", "Job 42:7", "Rom 8:19-28", "Heb 2:14-18", "Jas 1:13-17", "Gen 50:20", "Acts 10:38", "Rev 21:4", "Rom 12:15"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Oord: “Essential kenosis says God cannot override, withdraw, or fail to provide the power of freedom, agency, or existence to creation.” He adds: “I’m saying God can’t control others, not just won’t control.” (A debated view.)", source: "Thomas Jay Oord, “Who God Is: Essential Kenosis” (2019)", url: "https://thomasjayoord.com/who-god-is-essential-kenosis/" },
      { text: "Oord: “The God of essential kenosis can’t prevent evil by acting alone. So God is not to blame.”", source: "Thomas Jay Oord, “Who God Is: Essential Kenosis” (2019)", url: "https://thomasjayoord.com/who-god-is-essential-kenosis/" },
      { text: "Irenaeus: “For the glory of God is a living man; and the life of man consists in beholding God.” God’s aim is human life, not human ruin.", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies IV.20.7 (ANF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103420.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "need-behind-sin",
    q: "The relational gospel says that when we find the need behind a sin and the identity issue under it, we can overcome that sin. But Scripture says Christ died for our sins and delivered us. Why then do we need this?",
    short: "Christ’s finished work is the foundation; nothing we do adds to it. Finding the need and the identity lie behind a sin is not a second saviour. It is one way we learn to live from what Christ has already done, the way Paul tells us to “reckon” it true and be renewed in our minds.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "Both are true, at different levels",
        body: [
          "Scripture is clear: Christ “died for our sins” (1 Cor 15:3), our old self “was crucified with him” (Rom 6:6), and God “delivered us out of the power of darkness” (Col 1:13). In the relational gospel this is objective and finished. It happened to humanity in Christ before we did anything.",
          "But the same letters that say “you died” also say “put to death” (Col 3:3, 5), and the same chapter that says “our old man was crucified” says “reckon yourselves to be dead to sin” (Rom 6:6, 11). Paul does not see a contradiction. What is true <em>in Christ</em> has to be received, believed and lived <em>in us</em>. Theologians call this the difference between what Christ accomplished and how we participate in it.",
          "So looking at the need behind sin is not an extra to the cross. It is part of “be transformed by the renewing of your mind” (Rom 12:2) and “be renewed in the spirit of your mind” (Eph 4:23)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What many were taught",
        body: [
          "Many believers were taught: “You are forgiven, so just stop it. If you still struggle, pray harder, try harder, or doubt your salvation.” Others were taught: “It is finished, so your behaviour doesn’t matter.” The first produces shame and hiding. The second produces stuck people who never heal.",
          "Romans 7 describes the experience many know: “the good which I desire, I don’t do; but the evil which I don’t desire, that I practice” (Rom 7:19). Whether Paul describes his life before or after Christ is debated, but the experience is real. Willpower alone does not reach the root."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Scripture in context: sin, desire and identity",
        body: [
          "The common Greek word for sin, <em>hamartia</em>, carries the picture of missing the mark. Sin is often a real, God-given longing (for comfort, honour, safety, love) aimed at the wrong target. James traces sin back to desire (<em>epithymia</em>, Jas 1:14-15). The first temptation was an identity attack: “you will be like God” (Gen 3:5), offered to people who were <em>already</em> made in God’s image (Gen 1:27). The lie was that they lacked something.",
          "Jesus regularly went beneath the behaviour. To the Samaritan woman he spoke about thirst before he mentioned her marriages (John 4:10-18). Zacchaeus changed after Jesus welcomed him, not before (Luke 19:5-8). Jesus’ own temptations targeted identity: “If you are the Son of God…” (Matt 4:3), right after the Father had said “This is my beloved Son” (Matt 3:17).",
          "The early church saw Christ healing every part of us, not only cancelling a debt. Gregory of Nazianzus wrote that what Christ did not assume he did not heal. He took a full human mind and will so that our minds and desires could be healed from the inside."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer",
        body: [
          "The cross deals with sin and its guilt fully. Union with Christ gives us a new identity: beloved, included, a new creation (2 Cor 5:17; Gal 2:20). Finding the need behind a sin helps us see <em>which lie</em> we are still believing and <em>which longing</em> we are trying to meet apart from God. Then we bring that exact place to Christ and let truth replace the lie (John 8:32).",
          "This is not technique replacing grace. It is grace getting specific. The Spirit “searches” the heart (Rom 8:27; Ps 139:23-24) and leads us into truth (John 16:13). Knowing the need does not excuse the sin; it explains it, so that it can be healed rather than only suppressed."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The fair counter-view and a response",
        body: [
          "Many evangelical and Reformed teachers worry that “need behind sin” language psychologises sin, turns it into a victim story and weakens personal responsibility and repentance. They say the deepest problem is not unmet needs but a heart that loves self over God (Jer 17:9), and that the remedy is repentance and the Spirit’s power, not insight.",
          "That warning is worth hearing. Needs never justify harm, and repentance (<em>metanoia</em>, change of mind and direction) remains essential. But the two approaches can work together: real repentance includes turning from the lie about God and ourselves, not only from the act. And the Spirit’s power usually works through truth that we understand and receive (John 17:17)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "When you fall, do not hide; come into the light, because there is no condemnation in Christ (Rom 8:1; 1 John 1:7-9). Ask gently: What was I feeling just before? What was I hoping this would give me? What did I believe about myself or God in that moment?",
          "Then name the truth from Scripture about your identity, and look for a healthy, God-given way to meet the real need (rest, friendship, honest conversation, help). Confess to a trusted person (Jas 5:16). For deep or addictive patterns, seek pastoral and professional help; that is not a lack of faith."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["1 Cor 15:3", "Rom 6:6-11", "Col 3:3-10", "Rom 12:2", "Eph 4:22-24", "Rom 7:19", "Jas 1:14-15", "Gen 3:5", "John 4:10-18", "Luke 19:5-8", "Matt 3:17", "Matt 4:3", "2 Cor 5:17", "Rom 8:1", "Jas 5:16"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Gregory of Nazianzus: “For that which He has not assumed He has not healed; but that which is united to His Godhead is also saved.”", source: "Gregory of Nazianzus, To Cledonius (Ep. 101) (NPNF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3103a.htm" },
      { text: "Aquinas: “grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.” Grace works through our real human faculties, not around them.", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.1, a.8, reply to obj. 2", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1001.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "prayer-supernatural",
    q: "Like Pentecostals say, can’t we overcome sin and problems by prayer and the supernatural?",
    short: "Yes, prayer and the Spirit’s power are real, and Pentecostals rightly refuse a powerless faith. But in Scripture the supernatural usually works with ordinary means, growth and wisdom, not instead of them. God heals and delivers, and he also forms us slowly.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "What Pentecostals get right",
        body: [
          "The New Testament expects the Spirit to act. Believers are filled with the Spirit (Acts 2:4; Eph 5:18), receive gifts of healing and miracles (1 Cor 12:9-10), and are told to “walk by the Spirit, and you won’t fulfill the lust of the flesh” (Gal 5:16). Jesus cast out demons and healed (Acts 10:38). A faith that never expects God to do anything is not New Testament faith.",
          "Pentecostal and charismatic Christians have carried this hope to the whole church, and many people testify to real freedom through prayer, deliverance and healing. That deserves honour, not mockery."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Where it can go wrong",
        body: [
          "Problems start when prayer and the supernatural are treated as the <em>only</em> tools. Then people with trauma, addiction or illness are told to “just pray” or are prayed over for deliverance while the root wounds are never touched. When change does not come, they are told they lacked faith. That adds shame to suffering.",
          "Another risk is “spiritual bypassing”: using spiritual experiences to skip grief, honest conversation, repair or medical care. A powerful meeting can be a real touch from God and still not replace the slow work of forming new habits and healing relationships."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Scripture in context: supernatural and ordinary together",
        body: [
          "Look at how the Bible actually pictures change. The “fruit of the Spirit” (Gal 5:22-23) is fruit: it grows over seasons. Paul prayed three times for his “thorn” to be removed, and God answered, “My grace is sufficient for you” (2 Cor 12:7-9). Paul told Timothy to take a little wine for his stomach (1 Tim 5:23) and left Trophimus sick at Miletus (2 Tim 4:20). The same apostle who healed the sick did not treat every problem as needing a miracle.",
          "Nehemiah gives a simple model: “we made our prayer to our God, and set a watch against them day and night” (Neh 4:9). Jesus prayed all night (Luke 6:12) and also slept, ate, withdrew to rest and asked friends to stay with him (Mark 4:38; 6:31; 14:34). The Greek word for salvation, <em>sōtēria</em>, and the verb <em>sōzō</em> cover rescue, healing and wholeness; God uses many means to bring it."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer",
        body: [
          "In the relational gospel, the “supernatural” is not God breaking in from outside a world he is absent from. It is the Spirit who already indwells us, working in and through our bodies, minds, relationships and choices (Rom 8:11; Phil 2:13). Sometimes that work is sudden and visible. Often it is quiet and gradual. Both are God.",
          "So we do not choose between prayer and process. We pray because we live in union with God, and we take practical steps because that union includes our whole human life. As Aquinas put it, grace perfects nature rather than destroying it."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The fair counter-view and a response",
        body: [
          "Some Pentecostals answer: “You are lowering expectations. Jesus said believers would do greater works (John 14:12). Talking about process gives people an excuse not to believe.” Cessationists, from the other side, say miraculous gifts ended with the apostles, so the main means of change are Scripture, sacraments and discipline.",
          "A fair middle answer: expect God to act, and also honour how he usually acts. Testimonies of instant freedom are real, and so are the many faithful people who prayed and still needed years of healing. Neither group has less faith. If one approach does not work for you, that is not a verdict on your worth or your faith."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "Pray boldly and specifically, and invite others to pray with you (Jas 5:14-16). At the same time, take the next practical step: remove easy access to what traps you, find accountability, get counselling, see a doctor, rest. None of these is unspiritual.",
          "If someone tells you a problem continues because of your weak faith or a hidden demon, test that claim with care and wise, trusted people. Never stop needed medical treatment because of a prayer promise."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Acts 2:4", "Gal 5:16", "Gal 5:22-23", "1 Cor 12:9-10", "2 Cor 12:7-9", "1 Tim 5:23", "2 Tim 4:20", "Neh 4:9", "Luke 6:12", "Mark 6:31", "Rom 8:11", "Phil 2:13", "John 14:12", "Jas 5:14-16"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Aquinas: “grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.”", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.1, a.8, reply to obj. 2", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1001.htm" },
      { text: "Ignatius of Loyola (letter to Francis Borgia, 1555): he considered it an error to trust in human means alone, nor safe “to trust the whole matter to God our Lord without desiring to help myself by what he has given me”, so “I ought to make use of both parts.”", source: "Trent Horn, “St. Ignatius Said What?”, Catholic Answers (2019)", url: "https://www.catholic.com/magazine/online-edition/st-ignatius-said-what" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "ot-without-sin",
    q: "Could Old Testament people live without sinning (for example Luke 1:6, Phil 3:6, Job 1:1)? If they could, why did we need Jesus?",
    short: "“Blameless” in these verses means a life of integrity that no one could fairly accuse, including faithful use of the sacrifices for sin. It does not mean sinless. And Jesus did not come only to fix a rule-keeping problem: he came to unite humanity with God, give the Spirit, defeat death and reveal the Father.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "What the verses say",
        body: [
          "Zechariah and Elizabeth “were both righteous before God, walking blamelessly in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord” (Luke 1:6). Job “was blameless and upright, and one who feared God, and turned away from evil” (Job 1:1). Paul says of his past: “concerning the righteousness which is in the law, found blameless” (Phil 3:6).",
          "These are strong words, and we should not explain them away. Scripture really does say that people under the old covenant could live with deep integrity and covenant faithfulness. Grace and faith were already at work before Christ came in the flesh (Gen 15:6; Heb 11)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The original words: blameless is not sinless",
        body: [
          "The Greek word in Luke 1:6 and Phil 3:6 is <em>amemptos</em>, “without blame, deserving no censure”. In the Greek Old Testament it translates the Hebrew <em>tam</em> used of Job (Job 1:1, 8). It describes someone against whom no legitimate charge can be brought. It does not claim they never sinned.",
          "The Law itself expected sin and provided for it. It had offerings for sin and guilt (Lev 4–5) and the Day of Atonement (Lev 16). A “blameless” Israelite was one who kept the covenant, including using those provisions. Job offered sacrifices in case his children had sinned (Job 1:5), and at the end he says, “I…repent in dust and ashes” (Job 42:6). Zechariah, though blameless, doubted the angel and was made silent (Luke 1:18-20). Paul, “blameless” in the law, was at the same time persecuting the church (Phil 3:6; 1 Tim 1:13-15).",
          "So the same Old Testament that calls some people blameless also says, “there is no man who doesn’t sin” (1 Kings 8:46; Eccl 7:20), and Paul sums it up: “all have sinned, and fall short of the glory of God” (Rom 3:23)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Why Jesus, then?",
        body: [
          "Notice what Romans 3:23 says we fall short of: not merely a rulebook, but “the glory of God” (Greek <em>doxa</em>). Humanity was made for glory, for sharing God’s own life (2 Pet 1:4; Rom 8:29-30). Even perfect rule-keeping would not achieve that. Paul counted his blameless record as “loss” compared with “knowing Christ Jesus” (Phil 3:7-9).",
          "In the relational gospel, Jesus came to do what the law could never do. He joined God and humanity in his own person (John 1:14), so that we are united to God, not just forgiven. He gave a new heart and the indwelling Spirit, which the prophets promised as something new (Jer 31:31-34; Ezek 36:26-27). He defeated death and the fear of death (Heb 2:14-15). And he revealed the Father’s face, which no one had seen (John 1:18; 14:9). Irenaeus summed up the goal: “the glory of God is a living man; and the life of man consists in beholding God.”",
          "Even the best Old Testament saints were waiting for this (Luke 2:25-32; Heb 11:39-40). Zechariah himself, the “blameless” priest, sang with joy about the coming Saviour (Luke 1:68-79)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The fair counter-views",
        body: [
          "In the early 400s, Pelagius taught that sinless living was possible by human free will; Augustine strongly opposed him, and the Council of Carthage (418) rejected Pelagius’s teaching. Most Christians since then hold that no one apart from Christ has lived without sin. Reformed theology adds that Jesus came above all to bear the penalty of sin and to supply a perfect righteousness credited to believers (Rom 5:19; 2 Cor 5:21).",
          "Relational-gospel teachers do not deny forgiveness or Christ’s perfect obedience. They say the deepest reason for Jesus is not only a legal shortfall but a relational and ontological one: humanity needed to be healed and brought into the life of the Trinity. Many Orthodox and Catholic Christians frame it similarly. These views overlap more than they compete."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What this means for you",
        body: [
          "Do not despise integrity; “blameless” living is still a good goal (Phil 2:15). But do not measure your relationship with God by a spotless record. Even the best record falls short of what God actually wants to give you: himself.",
          "When you read about faithful people in the Old Testament, let it encourage you that grace was always at work. Then look to Jesus as the fulfilment of everything they hoped for."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Luke 1:6", "Job 1:1", "Job 1:5", "Job 42:6", "Phil 3:6-9", "Lev 4–5", "Lev 16", "1 Kings 8:46", "Eccl 7:20", "Rom 3:23", "Jer 31:31-34", "Ezek 36:26-27", "Heb 2:14-15", "John 1:18", "2 Pet 1:4", "Luke 1:68-79"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Thayer’s lexicon defines amemptos as “blameless, deserving no censure… free from fault or defect” (Luke 1:6; Phil 3:6), and notes that in the Greek Old Testament it renders Hebrew tam (Job 1:1, 8). Bible Hub’s topical note adds that it “does not teach sinless perfection on earth but the absence of grounds for accusation”.", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 273 ἄμεμπτος", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/273.htm" },
      { text: "Irenaeus: “For the glory of God is a living man; and the life of man consists in beholding God.”", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies IV.20.7 (ANF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103420.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "prayer-misunderstood",
    q: "Where did Christians misunderstand prayer? Some just pray and do nothing, while others focus on doing everything themselves.",
    short: "Both errors split what God joined: prayer and action. Prayer is not a way to hand off our part, and action is not a way to do without God. In the relational gospel, prayer is shared life with God, and that life naturally flows into wise, loving action.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "Two opposite mistakes",
        body: [
          "<strong>Prayer without action.</strong> Some believers pray about a job but never apply, pray for a marriage but never change how they speak, pray for the hungry but never share food. James is blunt: if a brother is hungry and you say “Go in peace, be warmed and filled” but give nothing, “what good is it?” (Jas 2:15-16). In the 17th century a movement called Quietism pushed passive prayer so far that the Catholic Church condemned it.",
          "<strong>Action without prayer.</strong> Others work as if everything depends on them. Prayer becomes a quick blessing before the “real” work. This often comes from fear, from a culture of self-reliance, or from being let down by God before. The popular saying “God helps those who help themselves” is not in the Bible, and much of Scripture says the opposite (Ps 127:1; John 15:5)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Scripture in context: prayer and action together",
        body: [
          "At the Red Sea, God said to Moses, “Why do you cry to me? Speak to the children of Israel, that they go forward” (Exod 14:15). Nehemiah “made our prayer to our God, and set a watch” (Neh 4:9). In Gethsemane Jesus told his friends to “watch and pray” (Mark 14:38), and then he got up and walked into what was coming.",
          "Paul joins them in one sentence: “work out your own salvation with fear and trembling. For it is God who works in you both to will and to work” (Phil 2:12-13). The Greek verb for God working, <em>energeō</em>, is the root of our word “energy”: God’s energy shows up in our willing and doing. We are “God’s fellow workers” (1 Cor 3:9)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "A famous saying, and what it teaches",
        body: [
          "Many people quote “Pray as though everything depended on God; act as though everything depended on you” and credit it to Ignatius of Loyola. Catholic apologist Trent Horn shows that this exact wording does not appear in Ignatius’s writings, and that a reversed version also circulates with uncertain origins. What Ignatius did write, in a 1555 letter, is that it is an error to trust in human means alone, and also unsafe to leave everything to God without using what he has given us: “I ought to make use of both parts.”",
          "That is close to the relational picture. Prayer and work are not two separate departments, one for God and one for us. Both happen in God."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer",
        body: [
          "Prayer in the relational gospel is not mainly a method to get things. It is sharing life with the Father, through the Son, in the Spirit (Rom 8:15, 26; Gal 4:6). When we pray, we are not trying to wake up a distant God. We are joining what the Son and Spirit are already praying (Heb 7:25; Rom 8:26-27).",
          "Out of that union, action grows naturally. Prayer changes what we see, what we want and what we are willing to do. Augustine said God asks us to pray not because he needs information but so that our desire is enlarged to receive what he wants to give. A person whose desire has been enlarged by God will usually find their hands moving too."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Fair views from different traditions",
        body: [
          "Contemplative traditions (Catholic, Orthodox, many Anglicans) warn activists that work without stillness becomes ego and burnout (Ps 46:10; Luke 10:38-42). Evangelical and Wesleyan traditions warn contemplatives that prayer must bear fruit in mission and holiness (Jas 2:17; Matt 25:35-40). Pentecostals stress expectant prayer for God to act in power.",
          "Each warning is right about the other’s danger. The answer is not to pick a side but to let prayer and action correct and fill each other, as they did in Jesus."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "If you tend to pray and wait: after you pray, ask, “What is one step I can take today?” and take it. If you tend to rush: before you act, stop for two minutes, breathe, and tell God honestly what you fear and want. Bring every plan into conversation with him.",
          "In danger or abuse, prayer never replaces getting safe. Like Nehemiah, pray and set a guard: tell someone, get help, and contact local emergency services if needed."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Jas 2:15-17", "Ps 127:1", "John 15:5", "Exod 14:15", "Neh 4:9", "Mark 14:38", "Phil 2:12-13", "1 Cor 3:9", "Rom 8:26-27", "Heb 7:25", "Luke 10:38-42", "Matt 25:35-40"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "The popular “pray as though everything depended on God…” maxim: in that precise form “the thought is nowhere found in Ignatius’s writings nor in any contemporary documents”; its earliest found citation is from 1868. Horn also notes 17th-century Quietists “prayed as if everything depended on God and did nothing else.”", source: "Trent Horn, “St. Ignatius Said What?”, Catholic Answers (2019)", url: "https://www.catholic.com/magazine/online-edition/st-ignatius-said-what" },
      { text: "Ignatius to Francis Borgia (1555): “I consider it an error to trust and hope in any means or efforts in themselves alone; nor do I consider it a safe path to trust the whole matter to God our Lord without desiring to help myself by what he has given me.”", source: "Quoted in Trent Horn, “St. Ignatius Said What?”, Catholic Answers", url: "https://www.catholic.com/magazine/online-edition/st-ignatius-said-what" },
      { text: "Augustine: God asks us to pray “not that thereby our wish may be intimated to Him… but in order that by prayer there may be exercised in us by supplications that desire by which we may receive what He prepares to bestow.”", source: "Augustine, Letter 130 to Proba, §17 (NPNF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1102130.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "what-works",
    q: "What counts as the “works” Scripture means when it says “you are not saved by works”?",
    short: "“Works” in these texts means anything we offer to earn, secure or prove our place with God: religious markers, moral records, performance and status. It does not mean love, obedience or effort that flows from grace. Scholars debate how much Paul’s phrase “works of the law” is about Jewish identity markers; both readings agree we cannot buy belonging.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "The key texts",
        body: [
          "“For by grace you have been saved through faith, and that not of yourselves; it is the gift of God, not of works, that no one would boast. For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works” (Eph 2:8-10). Notice: “not of works” and “for good works” sit side by side. Works are not the enemy; they are just not the root.",
          "Paul also says “a man is justified by faith apart from the works of the law” (Rom 3:28) and that we are “not justified by the works of the law but through faith in Jesus Christ” (Gal 2:16). Titus 3:5 adds “not by works of righteousness, which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy”."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What many were taught, and the scholarly debate",
        body: [
          "The traditional Protestant (Lutheran and Reformed) reading says “works” means any human effort to earn God’s favour, and Paul opposed a legalistic religion of merit. Justification is God declaring sinners righteous because of Christ, received by faith alone.",
          "Since the 1970s–80s the “New Perspective on Paul” (E.P. Sanders, James Dunn, N.T. Wright) has argued that first-century Judaism was mostly a religion of grace (“covenantal nomism”: you enter the covenant by grace and stay in by obedience), so “works of the law” in Romans and Galatians mainly means the Jewish boundary markers (circumcision, Sabbath, food laws) that separated Jews from Gentiles. Paul’s point, on this view, is that Gentiles do not have to become Jews to belong to God’s people (Gal 2:11-16).",
          "Reformed critics reply that some Jewish texts of the time do show works-righteousness, that Paul speaks of “works” about Abraham and David where boundary markers are not in view (Rom 4:1-8), and that texts like Eph 2:8-10 and Titus 3:5 clearly contrast grace with human merit. This debate is ongoing among serious scholars."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer: what counts as “works”",
        body: [
          "Both readings point to the same heart issue: using anything as a ticket to belong to God. In the relational gospel, “works” in the negative sense includes: religious markers (rituals, denominational badges, the right vocabulary); moral records (“I’m better than them”); spiritual performance (enough prayer, enough fasting, enough feelings in worship); and even faith treated as a work, as if your belief-score earns God’s love.",
          "What does <em>not</em> count: faith as trust in God’s faithfulness; repentance as turning toward the Father; love, generosity and obedience that flow from being loved (Gal 5:6, “faith working through love”); and hard effort in growth (1 Cor 15:10, “I worked more than all of them; yet not I, but the grace of God”). The difference is not whether you act, but whether you act <em>to get in</em> or <em>because you are in</em>.",
          "Our belonging rests on what God did in Christ for all humanity (2 Cor 5:14-19). Works do not make that true; they let it shine (Matt 5:16)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Fair views from other traditions",
        body: [
          "James says “faith, if it has no works, is dead” (Jas 2:17) and “by works a man is justified, and not only by faith” (Jas 2:24). Protestants usually read this as works being the evidence of real faith. Catholic teaching holds that good works done in grace truly cooperate in our growth in justification. Orthodox Christians speak of synergy: God’s grace and human response working together in theosis.",
          "The relational gospel can honour all of these: grace always comes first and is never earned, and grace is not passive. It is God’s own life working in us, producing real fruit."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "Check your motive gently: “Am I doing this to be accepted, or because I am accepted?” If it is the first, stop and receive again that you are loved in Christ (Rom 5:8). Then do the same good thing from rest.",
          "Watch for new boundary markers in your own circle (music style, politics, spiritual jargon, a certain experience) that quietly decide who is “in”. Paul resisted Peter publicly when Peter withdrew from Gentile believers over such markers (Gal 2:11-14). Welcome people as Christ welcomed you (Rom 15:7)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Eph 2:8-10", "Rom 3:28", "Gal 2:16", "Titus 3:5", "Rom 4:1-8", "Gal 2:11-16", "Gal 5:6", "1 Cor 15:10", "2 Cor 5:14-19", "Jas 2:17-24", "Phil 3:4-9", "Rom 15:7"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "The New Perspective “define[s] ‘works of the law’ (e.g., Rom. 3:28; Gal. 2:16) as primarily emphasizing three Jewish boundary markers: Sabbath, circumcision, and food laws”; the leading scholars named are E.P. Sanders, James D.G. Dunn and N.T. Wright. The essay (a Reformed critique) argues Paul also opposes works-righteousness more broadly.", source: "Robert J. Cara, “Justification and the New Perspective on Paul”, The Gospel Coalition", url: "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/essay/justification-new-perspective-paul/" },
      { text: "Sanders’s “covenantal nomism”: “One enters the covenant by election/grace and stays in by obedience to the law.”", source: "Robert J. Cara, “Justification and the New Perspective on Paul”, The Gospel Coalition", url: "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/essay/justification-new-perspective-paul/" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "only-when-ask",
    q: "Will God only help us when we ask? Why?",
    short: "No. Scripture shows God acting first, and acting for people who never asked: he seeks, gives rain to all, and sent Christ while we were still sinners. Yet he also invites us to ask, because asking is how love includes us, enlarges our hearts and makes us partners rather than spectators.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "God acts before we ask",
        body: [
          "Jesus says, “your Father knows what things you need, before you ask him” (Matt 6:8). God “makes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust” (Matt 5:45). Adam and Eve did not ask to be found; God came looking: “Where are you?” (Gen 3:9). The shepherd goes after the lost sheep that never called him (Luke 15:4).",
          "The biggest gift of all was unasked: “God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us” (Rom 5:8). Isaiah even hears God say, “before they call, I will answer” (Isa 65:24). So any idea that God sits idle until we pray the right words does not match the Bible."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "But Scripture also says “ask”",
        body: [
          "James says, “ye have not, because ye ask not” (Jas 4:2, KJV). Jesus says, “Ask, and it will be given you” (Matt 7:7), and told a parable “that they must always pray, and not give up” (Luke 18:1). Elijah prayed and rain came (Jas 5:17-18).",
          "Note the context of James 4:2: people were fighting and grasping to get what they wanted instead of bringing their desires to God, and in the next verse some asked with wrong motives (Jas 4:3). It is not a rule that God is stuck until asked; it is an invitation to stop grabbing and start trusting.",
          "Luke 18:1-8 is often misread. The unjust judge is a <em>contrast</em> with God, not a picture of him: if even a bad judge finally helps, “won’t God do justice for his chosen ones?” (Luke 18:7). You do not have to wear God down."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Why ask, then?",
        body: [
          "Augustine answered this 1,600 years ago. God does not need us to inform him; he asks us to pray so that our desire is “exercised” and enlarged: “His gifts are very great, but we are small and straitened in our capacity of receiving.” Prayer widens us to receive.",
          "Thomas Aquinas added that God has chosen to bring about some things <em>through</em> our prayers, as he brings about some things through our work. We pray “not that we may change the Divine disposition”, but to obtain what God has arranged to give through prayer. Our asking is a real part of how God works in the world.",
          "The relational gospel puts it simply: love does not force. A loving parent often gives without being asked, and also delights when a child comes and asks, talks and shares in what the parent is doing. Asking is not a fee; it is fellowship. God includes us as partners (1 Cor 3:9) rather than treating us as objects he manages."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Fair views from different traditions",
        body: [
          "Classical and Reformed theology says God has ordained both the ends and the means, including our prayers; prayer is not pointless, but it does not change God’s eternal plan. Open and relational theologians say prayer can genuinely change what happens, because God responds to us in real time. Some Pentecostal and Word of Faith teachers say God has given believers authority and “waits” for us to pray and speak.",
          "The relational gospel agrees with all of them that prayer matters. It is wary only of any version that makes God reluctant, passive or needing to be persuaded to love. God is already for us (Rom 8:31-32). Prayer is how we join him."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "Ask freely and honestly, as a child, for big and small things (Phil 4:6). Also thank God for the many things he gave that you never asked for. When an answer does not come as you hoped, do not conclude you asked wrongly or that God did not care; keep talking with him.",
          "And when you cannot pray at all, rest in this: “the Spirit himself makes intercession for us” (Rom 8:26). God’s help is not on hold until you find the words."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 6:8", "Matt 5:45", "Gen 3:9", "Luke 15:4", "Rom 5:8", "Isa 65:24", "Jas 4:2-3", "Matt 7:7-11", "Luke 18:1-8", "Jas 5:17-18", "Rom 8:26", "Rom 8:31-32", "Phil 4:6"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Augustine: “His gifts are very great, but we are small and straitened in our capacity of receiving.” Prayer exercises the desire “by which we may receive what He prepares to bestow.”", source: "Augustine, Letter 130 to Proba, §17 (NPNF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1102130.htm" },
      { text: "Aquinas: “For we pray not that we may change the Divine disposition, but that we may impetrate that which God has disposed to be fulfilled by our prayers.” And: we pray “not in order to make known to Him our needs or desires but that we ourselves may be reminded of the necessity of having recourse to God’s help.”", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.83, a.2 and reply to obj. 1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3083.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "fear-faith",
    q: "Many preachers tell us not to fear. Does God act because we learn not to fear? Why does so much preaching urge us to hope, trust, not fear and not be confused — as if God works only after someone learns to trust or have faith?",
    short: "God does not wait for us to become fearless before he acts; again and again Jesus helps frightened, doubting people. Faith matters because it is how we receive and walk with God, not a fee that switches him on. “Do not fear” in Scripture is comfort from a present God, not a condition for his love.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "Jesus helps people who are afraid",
        body: [
          "In the storm, the disciples are terrified. Jesus says, “Why are you fearful, O you of little faith?” and then, in the very next words, “he got up, rebuked the wind and the sea, and there was a great calm” (Matt 8:26). He acted while their faith was small. When Peter sank, “Immediately Jesus stretched out his hand, took hold of him” (Matt 14:31). A father cried, “I believe. Help my unbelief!” and Jesus healed his son (Mark 9:24-27).",
          "Paul says, “If we are faithless, he remains faithful; for he can’t deny himself” (2 Tim 2:13). God’s faithfulness does not depend on ours. That is the foundation of the whole gospel: Christ died for us “while we were yet sinners” (Rom 5:8), not after we had learned to trust."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Then why does faith matter so much?",
        body: [
          "Scripture does link faith and receiving. In Nazareth Jesus “could do no mighty work there, except that he laid his hands on a few sick people, and healed them”, and he “marveled because of their unbelief” (Mark 6:5-6). Hebrews says “without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him” (Heb 11:6). Jesus often said, “your faith has made you well” (Mark 5:34).",
          "The Greek word <em>pistis</em> means trust, confidence and faithfulness. It is less like a coin we put in a machine and more like an open hand. Even in Nazareth Jesus still healed some. Unbelief there was not God refusing; it was people refusing to come (Mark 6:3). Faith is how we receive and cooperate; it is not what makes God loving.",
          "Paul also speaks of living “by faith in the Son of God” (Gal 2:20); the KJV reads “the faith of the Son of God”. Scholars debate whether Greek <em>pistis Christou</em> means our faith in Christ or Christ’s own faithfulness. Either way, our faith rests on his faithfulness first."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Why “do not fear” is everywhere",
        body: [
          "“Do not be afraid” appears again and again in Scripture, but look at how it is said. It usually comes with a presence: “Don’t be afraid, for I am with you” (Isa 41:10); “Cheer up! It is I! Don’t be afraid” (Matt 14:27). It is comfort, not a test. God is not saying “stop being scared or I won’t help”. He is saying “I am here, so you do not have to be ruled by fear.”",
          "John explains how fear actually leaves: “There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear… We love him, because he first loved us” (1 John 4:18-19). Love comes first; the loss of fear follows. You do not get rid of fear to earn love; love slowly dissolves fear.",
          "Preachers emphasise hope and trust partly for good reasons: fear distorts how we see God, ourselves and others, and it drives many bad choices (Gen 3:10; Matt 25:25). Helping people trust is real pastoral care. But when it becomes “God will only move once you stop doubting”, it turns faith into a work and leaves anxious people feeling blamed."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Fair views and a response",
        body: [
          "Word of Faith teaching (for example in some prosperity and healing ministries) often presents faith as a spiritual force that releases God’s power and fear as a force that blocks it. Critics in the evangelical, Reformed and Pentecostal mainstream say this makes God dependent on human mental states and blames the sick and poor. Classical theology stresses that God acts by his own free grace, often in spite of our weak faith.",
          "The relational gospel agrees that faith is important and that fear can block us from receiving. But it insists that God is not reluctant, and that weak faith and real fear are met with compassion, not withdrawal. Jesus’ rebuke “O you of little faith” was spoken by someone who was already acting to save them."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do with your fear and confusion",
        body: [
          "Do not hide fear or confusion from God; bring it to him honestly, like the Psalms do (Ps 56:3, “When I am afraid, I will put my trust in you”). Notice that verse admits fear and trust in the same breath.",
          "Feed trust by looking at who God is in Jesus, not by trying to squeeze out a feeling. Remember past faithfulness (Ps 77:11), stay with people who hold hope for you when you can’t, and take practical steps to calm your body: rest, breathe, get help. Anxiety that will not lift is not a spiritual failure; talking with a doctor or counsellor is wise."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 8:26", "Matt 14:27-31", "Mark 9:24-27", "2 Tim 2:13", "Rom 5:8", "Mark 6:5-6", "Heb 11:6", "Mark 5:34", "Gal 2:20", "Isa 41:10", "1 John 4:18-19", "Ps 56:3", "Ps 77:11"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "Aquinas on why prayer depends on faith: “it is through faith that man comes to know of God’s omnipotence and mercy, which are the source whence prayer impetrates what it asks for.” The source of the answer is God’s mercy; faith is how we know and receive it.", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.83, a.15, reply to obj. 3", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3083.htm" },
      { text: "Augustine: what we receive grows “in proportion to the simplicity of our faith, the firmness of our hope, and the ardour of our desire” — faith enlarges our capacity to receive, it does not create God’s willingness to give.", source: "Augustine, Letter 130 to Proba, §17 (NPNF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1102130.htm" }
    ]
  },

  {
    id: "god-tests",
    q: "Does God test us, according to the relational gospel?",
    short: "Scripture does say God “tested” people, but the words mean proving and refining, like testing gold, not setting traps. God never tempts anyone to evil and does not send abuse or disease to see if we pass. The relational gospel says God uses the trials of a broken world to reveal and strengthen trust, and he stays with us in them.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "What many were taught",
        body: [
          "Many believers were told, when a crisis hit, “God is testing you.” Sometimes this meant God sent the illness, the job loss or the betrayal to see how you would react. That can leave people wondering whether God is a kind examiner or a cruel one, and whether their pain is his doing.",
          "Others react the opposite way and say God never tests anyone. Scripture is more nuanced than either."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The original words",
        body: [
          "Hebrew <em>nasah</em> (Gen 22:1, “God tested Abraham”; Deut 8:2) and Greek <em>peirazō</em> both mean to test or try. <em>Peirazō</em> can mean “test” or “tempt”, and context decides which. In James 1:13 it clearly means enticing to sin: “Let no man say when he is tempted, ‘I am tempted by God,’ for God can’t be tempted by evil, and he himself tempts no one.”",
          "Another Greek word, <em>dokimazō</em>, means to prove or approve something by testing, as metal is assayed. Peter uses it: “the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire” (1 Pet 1:7). James says trials (<em>peirasmoi</em>) produce endurance, “that you may be perfect and complete” (Jas 1:2-4).",
          "So there are two different things. Temptation aims to make you fall, and it does not come from God. Testing in the good sense aims to reveal and strengthen what is real, the way Jesus “tested” Philip while already knowing what he would do (John 6:5-6)."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Close reading: Abraham and the wilderness",
        body: [
          "Deuteronomy 8:2 says God led Israel in the wilderness “to humble you, to test you, to know what was in your heart”. In context the same chapter says God fed them with manna, their clothes did not wear out, and he disciplined them “as a man disciplines his son” (Deut 8:3-5). The test was a journey with a present, providing Father, so they would learn to live “by every word that proceeds out of Yahweh’s mouth” (Deut 8:3).",
          "Genesis 22 is harder. Interpreters differ. Traditional readings see Abraham’s faith proven and a foreshadowing of God giving his own Son (Heb 11:17-19). Some scholars and relational readers also note that child sacrifice was practised around Abraham, and the story ends with God <em>stopping</em> the sacrifice and providing a ram (Gen 22:11-13): God reveals himself as one who does not want children sacrificed (see also Jer 19:5). These readings are interpretations, and they can sit together."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The relational answer",
        body: [
          "God is love, and in him there is no darkness (1 John 1:5; 4:8). He gives “every good gift” (Jas 1:17). He does not send abuse, sickness or disaster to grade us. Jesus never told a sick person God had made them ill to test them; he healed them (Matt 4:23). Hardship comes from a world that is still groaning (Rom 8:22), from human choices and from evil that God opposes.",
          "But God never wastes our pain. In trials he is present, he strengthens trust, he brings hidden things into the light so they can be healed, and he grows us into maturity (Rom 5:3-5; Heb 12:10-11). The Greek <em>paideia</em> in Hebrews 12 means child-training and education, not punishment. And God promises a way through: “God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able” (1 Cor 10:13).",
          "So the relational answer is: God tests in the sense of proving and refining, always as a loving Father, and always with us. God never tempts us to evil, and he does not cause evil in order to test us."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "The fair counter-view and a response",
        body: [
          "Reformed theology teaches that God sovereignly ordains trials, including painful ones, for our good and his glory (Rom 8:28; Gen 50:20; Job 1–2). In this view saying God “allows but does not send” is a distinction without a difference, and it comforts people to know nothing happens outside God’s plan.",
          "A fair response: many people do find comfort in that view, and Scripture clearly says God brings good out of evil. But Jesus is our clearest picture of God, and he treated sickness and oppression as things to overcome (Acts 10:38; Luke 13:16). In Job, it is the accuser, not God, who strikes Job (Job 1:12; 2:7), and God later rebukes the friends who explained Job’s suffering as divine punishment (Job 42:7). Christians can disagree here and still trust God’s goodness."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "Do not tell a suffering person “God is testing you”; it can sound like God is their enemy. Weep with them and help (Rom 12:15). In your own trials, ask: “Father, what do you want to show me or grow in me here?” rather than “What did I do to deserve this?”",
          "If a situation is abusive or dangerous, it is not a “test” to endure. Get safe, seek help from trusted people and professionals, and contact local emergency services if needed. God’s refining never requires you to stay in harm."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Gen 22:1-13", "Deut 8:2-5", "Jas 1:2-4", "Jas 1:13-17", "1 Pet 1:6-7", "John 6:5-6", "1 Cor 10:13", "Heb 12:10-11", "Rom 5:3-5", "Rom 8:22", "Job 1:12", "Job 2:7", "Job 42:7", "Jer 19:5", "Acts 10:38"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "On peirazō: “The word means either test or tempt”; “Context alone determines which sense is intended, or if both apply simultaneously.” It is used negatively in Jas 1:13-14 and positively in Jas 1:12 and 1 Cor 10:13. In the Septuagint it often renders Hebrew nasah (Gen 22:1).", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 3985 πειράζω (HELPS Word-studies and Topical Lexicon)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/3985.htm" },
      { text: "Thayer’s lexicon, on the Old Testament usage of peirazō for God: to bring trials “in order to prove his character and the steadfastness of his faith” (citing Gen 22:1; Exod 20:20; Deut 8:2), distinct from soliciting to sin (Jas 1:13).", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 3985 πειράζω (Thayer’s Greek Lexicon)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/3985.htm" }
    ]
  }
];

RG.lvEvidence4 = [
  { text: "Oord: “Essential kenosis says God cannot override, withdraw, or fail to provide the power of freedom, agency, or existence to creation. Consequently, God cannot control creatures or creation.” He presents this as his answer to the problem of evil; it is a debated, minority view.", source: "Thomas Jay Oord, “Who God Is: Essential Kenosis” (2019)", url: "https://thomasjayoord.com/who-god-is-essential-kenosis/" },
  { text: "Irenaeus: “For the glory of God is a living man; and the life of man consists in beholding God.”", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies IV.20.7 (ANF trans.)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103420.htm" },
  { text: "Gregory of Nazianzus: “For that which He has not assumed He has not healed; but that which is united to His Godhead is also saved.”", source: "Gregory of Nazianzus, To Cledonius (Ep. 101)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3103a.htm" },
  { text: "Aquinas: “grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.”", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.1, a.8, reply to obj. 2", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1001.htm" },
  { text: "Aquinas: “we pray not that we may change the Divine disposition, but that we may impetrate that which God has disposed to be fulfilled by our prayers.”", source: "Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.83, a.2", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3083.htm" },
  { text: "Augustine: God asks us to pray so “that by prayer there may be exercised in us by supplications that desire by which we may receive what He prepares to bestow. His gifts are very great, but we are small and straitened in our capacity of receiving.”", source: "Augustine, Letter 130 to Proba, §17", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1102130.htm" },
  { text: "The maxim “Pray as though everything depended on God; act as though everything depended on you” is not found in that form in Ignatius’s writings; its earliest known citation is from 1868. Ignatius’s own letter to Borgia (1555) says to “make use of both parts”: human means and trust in God.", source: "Trent Horn, “St. Ignatius Said What?”, Catholic Answers (2019)", url: "https://www.catholic.com/magazine/online-edition/st-ignatius-said-what" },
  { text: "New Perspective authors (Sanders, Dunn, Wright) read “works of the law” (Rom 3:28; Gal 2:16) as “primarily emphasizing three Jewish boundary markers: Sabbath, circumcision, and food laws”; Reformed critics argue Paul also opposes works-righteousness more broadly.", source: "Robert J. Cara, “Justification and the New Perspective on Paul”, The Gospel Coalition", url: "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/essay/justification-new-perspective-paul/" },
  { text: "Greek amemptos (Luke 1:6; Phil 3:6) means “blameless, deserving no censure”; in the Greek Old Testament it renders Hebrew tam, used of Job (Job 1:1, 8).", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 273 (Thayer’s Lexicon)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/273.htm" },
  { text: "Greek peirazō “means either test or tempt”, and “context alone determines which sense is intended”; James 1:13 uses it of enticement to sin, while 1 Cor 10:13 and Jas 1:12 use it of trials.", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 3985 (HELPS Word-studies)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/3985.htm" }
];
